{"name":"Peachy Feeling","hex":"#ed8666","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peachy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Resembling a peach, peach-like.","Very good, excellent, typically used sarcastically to indicate a state of misery, resentment or great frustration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peachy"},{"term":"feeling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Emotionally sensitive.","Expressive of great sensibility; attended by, or evincing, sensibility."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Sensation, particularly through the skin.","Emotion; impression."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feeling"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peachy","blocks":[{"text":"\"Peachy\" is a song by Australian singer-songwriter Missy Higgins and the third single released from her second album, On A Clear Night. The track features a guest appearance by Neil Finn playing electric guitar. The single was released on 10 November 2007, as a digital download only. At the ARIA Music Awards of 2008 Higgins was nominated for Best Female Artist for this single."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/71/MissyHiggins_Peachy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peachy","wikidata":"Q7157725"},{"title":"Feeling","blocks":[{"text":"According to the APA Dictionary of Psychology, a feeling is \"a self-contained phenomenal experience\"; feelings are \"subjective, evaluative, and independent of the sensations, thoughts, or images evoking them\". The term feeling is closely related to, but not the same as, emotion. Feeling may, for instance, refer to the conscious subjective experience of emotions. The study of subjective experiences is called phenomenology. Psychotherapy generally involves a therapist helping a client understand, articulate, and learn to effectively regulate the client's own feelings, and ultimately to take responsibility for the client's experience of the world. Feelings are sometimes held to be characteristic of embodied consciousness."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feeling","wikidata":"Q205555"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Interoception_and_the_body.png/500px-Interoception_and_the_body.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Interoception_and_the_body.png/1280px-Interoception_and_the_body.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Interoception_and_the_body.png/500px-Interoception_and_the_body.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInteroception_and_the_body.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Emotions_-_3.png/330px-Emotions_-_3.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Emotions_-_3.png/330px-Emotions_-_3.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Examples of six basic emotions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmotions_-_3.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Sensitiva_%28Miquel_Blay%2C_MRABASF_E-76%29_01.jpg/500px-Sensitiva_%28Miquel_Blay%2C_MRABASF_E-76%29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Sensitiva_%28Miquel_Blay%2C_MRABASF_E-76%29_01.jpg/1280px-Sensitiva_%28Miquel_Blay%2C_MRABASF_E-76%29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Sensitiva_%28Miquel_Blay%2C_MRABASF_E-76%29_01.jpg/500px-Sensitiva_%28Miquel_Bla